{"type":"assistant","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The issue was in `format_hms` in `repo/timefmt.py`. Minutes were computed as total minutes (`seconds // 60`), so anything at or past an hour kept growing the minute field (e.g. 3600s → `1h 60m 0s` instead of `1h 0m 0s`).\n\nMinutes are now taken from the remainder after hours: `(seconds % 3600) // 60`, so they stay in the 0–59 range and roll correctly into hours.\n\n`tests/visible/test_hms.py` passes, including the hour-boundary case at 3600 seconds."}]},"session_id":"95e4b30c-74b3-47a3-9aaf-689abf9a43c6"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","duration_ms":40802,"duration_api_ms":40802,"is_error":false,"result":"Searching the codebase for where durations are formatted.\nIdentifying the bug: minutes use `seconds // 60` instead of reducing modulo 60, so 3600s becomes \"60m\". Fixing it.\nRemoving the outdated bug comment now that it's fixed.\nThe issue was in `format_hms` in `repo/timefmt.py`. Minutes were computed as total minutes (`seconds // 60`), so anything at or past an hour kept growing the minute field (e.g. 3600s → `1h 60m 0s` instead of `1h 0m 0s`).\n\nMinutes are now taken from the remainder after hours: `(seconds % 3600) // 60`, so they stay in the 0–59 range and roll correctly into hours.\n\n`tests/visible/test_hms.py` passes, including the hour-boundary case at 3600 seconds.","session_id":"95e4b30c-74b3-47a3-9aaf-689abf9a43c6","request_id":"1f8b4905-982c-463f-8e35-006e0727f4fe","usage":{"inputTokens":11791,"outputTokens":1074,"cacheReadTokens":76390,"cacheWriteTokens":0}}
